# Sample Terminator key bindings. Copy to ~/.terminatorrc (or point
# $TERMINATORRC at a file) and restart Terminator, or change bindings at run
# time with the `bind` built-in, which saves them here:
#
#   bind                       list actions and their keys
#   bind ACTION KEY[, KEY...]  rebind (use `none` to unbind) and save
#   bind -d ACTION             back to the default and save
#   bind -r                    reload this file
#
# Format: action = key[, key...]  with Ctrl+, Alt+ and Shift+ modifiers and a
# single character or an X key name (Left, Home, Delete, F5, Page_Up ...).
# Invalid or conflicting lines are reported at startup and that action keeps
# its default. The defaults are shown below, commented out.

# backward-char     = Left
# forward-char      = Right
# beginning-of-line = Ctrl+A, Home
# end-of-line       = Ctrl+E, End
# previous-history  = Up
# next-history      = Down
# unix-line-discard = Ctrl+U
# kill-line         = Ctrl+K
# unix-word-rubout  = Ctrl+W
# delete-char       = Delete
# clear-screen      = Ctrl+L
# end-of-file       = Ctrl+D
# copy              = Ctrl+Shift+C
# paste             = Ctrl+Shift+V
# interrupt         = Ctrl+C
# suspend           = Ctrl+Z
# scroll-page-up    = Shift+Page_Up
# scroll-page-down  = Shift+Page_Down
# scroll-line-up    = Shift+Up
# scroll-line-down  = Shift+Down
# scroll-to-top     = Shift+Home
# scroll-to-bottom  = Shift+End
# new-tab           = Ctrl+Shift+T
# close-tab         = Ctrl+Shift+W
# next-tab          = Ctrl+Page_Down, Ctrl+Tab
# previous-tab      = Ctrl+Page_Up, Ctrl+Shift+Tab
#
# Not rebindable: Alt+1 ... Alt+9 show tab 1 ... 9 (unless bound above).
# Mouse on the tab bar: click a tab to show it, [ + ] for a new tab,
# middle-click a tab to close it, the wheel to go through the tabs.
# reverse-search-history = Ctrl+R
